The problem can be approached by categorizing numbers from 1 to 50 according to their remainders when divided by 7. For any pair of numbers to have a sum divisible by 7, the remainders of the numbers in each category must add up to 7. So, if a number \(n\) has a remainder \(r\) when divided by 7, there is exactly one other category with remainders \(7 - r\) to cover the sum. The numbers from 1 to 50 can be grouped into the following categories based on their remainders when divided by 7:

1. Remainder 0: {7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42, 49}
2. Remainder 1: {1, 8, 15, 22, 29, 36, 43, 50}
3. Remainder 2: {2, 9, 16, 23, 30, 37, 44}
4. Remainder 3: {3, 10, 17, 24, 31, 38, 45}
5. Remainder 4: {4, 11, 18, 25, 32, 39, 46}
6. Remainder 5: {5, 12, 19, 26, 33, 40, 47}
7. Remainder 6: {6, 13, 20, 27, 34, 41, 48}

For each pair of categories that sum up to (0, 0), (1, 6), (2, 5), (3, 4), we can include at most one set of numbers from each category in our final set \(S\). Notice that there are \(7, 8, 7, 7, 8, 8, 7\) numbers in these categories respectively, and we can take the maximum number from each pair summing to 7 (i.e., \(7 + 8 = 15 \times 2 + 7 + 7 + 8 = 14 + 7 + 8 + 7 = 36\), but we need to remove one from the pair that sums to 7, so \(14 + 7 + 7 + 8 = 36 - 1 = 35\)). Thus, we can take 7 from the first category (remainder 0), 8 from the second category (remainder 1), 7 from the third category (remainder 2), 7 from the fourth category (remainder 3), 8 from the fifth category (remainder 4), 7 from the sixth category (remainder 5), and 6 from the seventh category (remainder 6), giving a total of \(7 + 8 + 7 + 7 + 8 + 7 + 6 = 46 / 2 = 23\). </think> <answer>23</answer>

